Investigate this Wazuh alert:

{
  "severity": "critical",
  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "10.108.57.184", "inferred_role": "cluster_internal_attacker_source", "first_seen": "2026-03-30T01:11:02+0000", "last_seen": "2026-03-30T01:21:40+0000"},
    {"ip": "10.108.57.42", "inferred_role": "target_kubelet_node", "first_seen": "2026-03-30T01:11:02+0000", "last_seen": "2026-03-30T01:21:40+0000"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Discovery / Container Resource Discovery", "technique": "T1609", "what_happened": "At 2026-03-30 01:11:02 UTC, Suricata logged an HTTP request from data.srcip=10.108.57.184 to data.dstip=10.108.57.42 on data.dstport=10250 — the kubelet read-only/secured API port. The request was GET https://10.108.57.42:10250/pods with NO Authorization header (anonymous). The kubelet :10250 port should ONLY ever be reached by the kube-apiserver and node-local components; a request from an arbitrary pod/node IP (10.108.57.184) to /pods is an immediate misconfiguration-abuse signal. Suricata fired 'ET SCAN Kubernetes Kubelet API Access Attempt' on data.dstport=10250.", "chain_reason": "Suricata signature matching kubelet API access on data.dstport=10250 with data.srcip=10.108.57.184 not in the kube-apiserver allowlist. Wazuh rule 92703 'Anonymous kubelet API access' attached rule.mitre.id T1609.", "first_seen": "2026-03-30T01:11:02+0000"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Discovery / Container Resource Discovery", "technique": "T1609", "what_happened": "At 01:13:30 UTC (~2.5 min later), the same data.srcip=10.108.57.184 issued a second anonymous probe: GET https://10.108.57.42:10250/runningpods/ — enumerating actively-running workloads on the node. This is the kubelet equivalent of 'kubectl get pods' but bypassing the API server and RBAC entirely. Suricata again fired the kubelet API access signature on data.dstport=10250, data.url contained '/runningpods/'.", "chain_reason": "Second Suricata kubelet-port-10250 alert from the same data.srcip within the incident window, data.http.url path '/runningpods/'. Wazuh rule 92703 fired again — repeated anonymous enumeration of the same kubelet.", "first_seen": "2026-03-30T01:13:30+0000"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Execution / Container Administration Command", "technique": "T1609", "what_happened": "At 01:16:10 UTC (~2.5 min after step 2), data.srcip=10.108.57.184 sent an HTTP POST to https://10.108.57.42:10250/run/prod/redis-0/redis with body 'cmd=id' — the kubelet RCE endpoint that executes an arbitrary command inside the named container WITHOUT any authentication when anonymous-auth is enabled. This is the pivot from Discovery to EXECUTION: the attacker is no longer just listing pods, they are attempting to run a command inside the prod/redis-0 container. Suricata observed the POST to data.dstport=10250 with data.http.http_method=POST and data.url '/run/prod/redis-0/redis'. Wazuh rule 92704 'Kubelet remote code execution attempt' fired with rule.mitre.id T1609.", "chain_reason": "Suricata POST to data.dstport=10250 path '/run/prod/redis-0/redis' from data.srcip=10.108.57.184. Wazuh rule 92704 chained to the two prior rule 92703 events from the same source — Discovery → Execution progression on the same kubelet node within a 5-minute window.", "first_seen": "2026-03-30T01:16:10+0000"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2026-03-30T01:11:02+0000", "end": "2026-03-30T01:21:40+0000"},
  "alert_count": 3,
  "unique_steps": 3,
  "correlation_note": "Three Suricata alerts on data.dstport=10250 from a single cluster-internal source IP (10.108.57.184) against one kubelet node (10.108.57.42) within ~5 minutes, progressing /pods → /runningpods/ → POST /run/[ns]/[pod]/[ctr]. Wazuh correlation rule chained rule 92703 (x2) + rule 92704 into one T1609 Container Administration Command incident."
}
